{"file":{"content":"This is an excellent endpoint. The finite side has now been reduced to a **single annealed Fourier-decay statement**:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\operatorname{op{:}flat}:\\qquad\n\t\\sum_{u\\bmod 3^c}|\\widehat E_c(u)|^2\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c).\n}\n]\n\nThat is a major simplification. The Collatz-specific finite operator, character twists, coboundaries, cascades, and Schur bookkeeping have all been pushed behind one clean renewal estimate.\n\nMy main critique is now organizational: the paper should stop presenting “threshold cascade contraction” as the open problem. It is now a **conditional theorem from op:flat**, not the live endpoint. The live endpoint is exactly:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{threshold renewal Fourier flattening for }C_w\\bmod 3^c.\n}\n]\n\nEverything after that is already assembled.\n\n## 1. What changed\n\nYou tried to extend flattening by a fiber bound\n\n[\n\\sup_x \\mu(C_w=x)\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c)3^{-c}.\n]\n\nThat is false at threshold because\n\n[\n\\mu(C_w=-1)\\asymp 2^{-c},\n]\n\nwhich is much larger than\n\n[\n3^{-c}.\n]\n\nSo pointwise equidistribution fails. This is important: the law has heavy atoms.\n\nBut the (L^2) claim can still hold:\n\n[\n\\operatorname{Col}_c(c)\n=======================\n\n\\sum_x \\mu(x)^2\n\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c)3^{-c}.\n]\n\nThat is exactly the right correction. The distribution is not flat in (L^\\infty), but is flat enough in (L^2).\n\nThe current numerical evidence\n\n[\n3^c\\operatorname{Col}_c(c)\n==========================\n\n3.32,\\ 3.53,\\ 3.70,\\ 3.90\n]\n\nstrongly suggests\n\n[\n3^c\\operatorname{Col}_c(c)=O(c),\n]\n\nor at worst (\\operatorname{poly}(c)). That is precisely what the cascade proof needs.\n\n## 2. The right theorem to aim for\n\nState the remaining theorem as:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Threshold renewal Fourier theorem.}\n]\n\nLet\n\n[\nC_w=\\sum_{j=0}^{c-1}3^{c-1-j}2^{B_j(w)}\n\\pmod{3^c},\n]\n\nwhere the increments (b_j\\ge1) are independent with\n\n[\n\\Pr(b=j)=2^{-j}.\n]\n\nLet\n\n[\n\\widehat E_c(u)\n===============\n\n\\mathbb E_w\n\\exp\\left(\\frac{2\\pi i u C_w}{3^c}\\right).\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\sum_{u\\bmod3^c}|\\widehat E_c(u)|^2\n\t\\le c^A\n}\n]\n\nfor some absolute (A).\n\nThe conjecturally sharper result is probably\n\n[\n\\sum_u|\\widehat E_c(u)|^2=O(c),\n]\n\nbut do not aim for the sharp linear bound first. Polynomial suffices.\n\n## 3. Why the worst-case scale product cannot prove it\n\nYou found the key obstruction:\n\n[\n\\eta_1=\\frac1{\\sqrt3},\n\\qquad\n3\\eta_1^2=1.\n]\n\nThis is not a nuisance. It explains why the Plancherel sum grows linearly instead of staying bounded. At the shallowest conductor scale, damping is exactly balanced by the number of frequencies.\n\nAt deeper scales,\n\n[\n\\eta_k\\uparrow1,\n]\n\nso a worst-case product over scales is hopeless. The deep scales can look almost undamped if the phase is adversarially aligned.\n\nTherefore the proof cannot be:\n\n[\n\\text{for every path, multiply deterministic damping factors.}\n]\n\nIt must be:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{aligned tiny-phase episodes are transient under the expanding phase chain.}\n}\n]\n\nThat is the right endpoint.\n\n## 4. The phase-chain formulation\n\nThe proof should be built around the phase dynamics you identified:\n\n[\n\\theta\\longmapsto 3\\cdot 2^b\\theta\\pmod1.\n]\n\nThe dangerous set at conductor depth (d) is a small neighborhood of zero:\n\n[\n|\\theta|\\lesssim 3^{-d}.\n]\n\nIf an orbit stays in this region, damping is weak. But because the map expands roughly by a factor (3\\cdot2^b), the orbit should escape in (O(d)) steps, and after escape it encounters shallow-scale damping of strength approximately\n\n[\n\\eta_1=\\frac1{\\sqrt3}.\n]\n\nSo the theorem should look like a stopping-time estimate.\n\nDefine a level function\n\n[\n\\ell(\\theta)=\\max{d:|\\theta|_{\\mathbb T}\\le 3^{-d}}.\n]\n\nThen prove a drift estimate:\n\n[\n\\mathbb E[\\ell(\\theta_{n+1})\\mid \\theta_n=\\theta]\n\\le\n\\max(\\ell(\\theta)-1,0)+O(1)\n]\n\nor, more usefully, a weighted contraction estimate:\n\n[\n\\mathbb E\\bigl[V(\\theta_{n+1}),a(\\theta_n,b)^2\\bigr]\n\\le\nV(\\theta_n)-\\kappa,\\mathbf 1_{\\ell(\\theta_n)>C}\n]\n\nfor some Lyapunov weight (V). Here (a(\\theta,b)) is the single-step Fourier multiplier.\n\nThe goal is to show that along a (c)-step phase trajectory, the number of effectively damped steps is at least\n\n[\nc-O(\\log c)\n]\n\nor at least enough to give\n\n[\n|\\widehat E_c(u)|^2\n\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c),3^{-\\operatorname{level}(u)}.\n]\n\nSumming over levels then gives (\\operatorname{poly}(c)).\n\n## 5. A concrete sufficient bound\n\nDecompose frequencies by conductor level. Put\n\n[\nu=3^r u_0,\\qquad 3\\nmid u_0,\n]\n\nand\n\n[\nL=c-r.\n]\n\nThere are\n\n[\n\\asymp 3^L\n]\n\nfrequencies of level (L).\n\nTo prove\n\n[\n\\sum_u|\\widehat E_c(u)|^2\\le\\operatorname{poly}(c),\n]\n\nit is enough to prove, for level (L),\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\max_{v_3(u)=c-L}|\\widehat E_c(u)|^2\n\t\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c),3^{-L}.\n}\n]\n\nEquivalently,\n\n[\n|\\widehat E_c(u)|\n\\le\n\\operatorname{poly}(c),3^{-L/2}.\n]\n\nThis is exactly the sampled per-coefficient decay you report.\n\nSo the attack should be levelwise:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Levelwise Fourier decay.}\n]\n\nFor every (u) of conductor level (L),\n\n[\n|\\widehat E_c(u)|\\le Cc^A3^{-L/2}.\n]\n\nThen:\n\n[\n\\sum_{L=0}^c\n#{u:\\operatorname{level}(u)=L}\\cdot C^2c^{2A}3^{-L}\n\\le\nC'c^{2A+1}.\n]\n\nThis proves op:flat.\n\n## 6. The transience lemma you need\n\nA plausible core lemma is:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Tiny-phase transience lemma.}\n]\n\nLet\n\n[\n\\theta_{n+1}=3\\cdot2^{b_n}\\theta_n\\pmod1,\n\\qquad \\Pr(b_n=j)=2^{-j}.\n]\n\nFor an initial phase (\\theta_0) of conductor level (L), define\n\n[\nN_{\\mathrm{bad}}\n================\n\n#{0\\le n<c:|\\theta_n|\\le 3^{-2}\\text{ or lies in the weak-damping region}}.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\nN_{\\mathrm{bad}}\\le O(\\log c)+O(1)\n]\n\nin the weighted expectation relevant to the Fourier coefficient.\n\nMore quantitatively, prove:\n\n[\n\\mathbb E\\left[\n\\prod_{n<c} A(\\theta_n)^2\n\\right]\n\\le\nC c^A3^{-L},\n]\n\nwhere (A(\\theta)) is the one-step multiplier magnitude. This is the exact square-decay statement you need.\n\nThe difficulty is that (\\theta_n) itself depends on the random increments (b_n), so the product is not deterministic. But that is also the source of escape.\n\n## 7. Rephrase as a transfer operator on phase levels\n\nInstead of tracking (\\theta) continuously, discretize by (3)-adic level:\n\n[\nd_n=\\ell(\\theta_n).\n]\n\nFor a fixed level (d), the next level satisfies approximately\n\n[\nd_{n+1}\\approx d_n-1-b_n\\log_3 2\n]\n\nuntil it hits (0), except when wraparound or accidental near-integer alignment occurs.\n\nSince (b_n\\ge1), the expected level drop is positive. Accidental re-entry to a high level should cost a small factor (3^{-h}).\n\nSo build a comparison Markov chain on levels (d\\in{0,\\dots,L}) with transition probabilities\n\n[\nP(d\\to d')\\le C3^{-(d'-\\max(d-C,0))_+}\n]\n\nand damping weight\n\n[\n\\eta(d)^2.\n]\n\nThen prove the weighted transfer matrix has total mass\n\n[\n\\le C L^A3^{-L}.\n]\n\nThis would be a clean proof of levelwise Fourier decay.\n\nThe exact form of (\\eta(d)) may be:\n\n[\n\\eta(d)=\\left|\\sum_b2^{-b}e(2^b\\theta)\\right|\n]\n\nor the corresponding multiplier in your recurrence. Near (d=1),\n\n[\n\\eta(1)=1/\\sqrt3.\n]\n\nFor large (d), (\\eta(d)\\approx1-O(3^{-2d})), hence the need for transience.\n\n## 8. Known-method analogy, but not overclaim\n\nThis is very close in spirit to Fourier decay of self-similar or Bernoulli convolution measures: high frequencies are damped because the expanding dual orbit cannot remain near resonances too often.\n\nBut I would avoid saying “known theorem applies” unless you identify an exact one. The maps here are\n\n[\n\\theta\\mapsto3\\cdot2^b\\theta\n]\n\nwith a non-uniform random multiplier and a (3)-adic modulus. It is a solvable renewal-Fourier estimate, but not obviously a direct corollary of a standard Bernoulli-convolution theorem.\n\nSafe wording:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{op:flat is a Bernoulli-convolution-type Fourier decay problem,}\n}\n]\n\nnot:\n\n[\n\\text{it follows from Bernoulli convolution theory.}\n]\n\n## 9. What to compute next\n\nThe next computation should test the stopping-time lemma directly.\n\nFor each (c) and each frequency level (L):\n\n1. Sample or enumerate trajectories of the phase chain.\n2. Record the number of visits to weak-damping regions.\n3. Record escape time from initial size (3^{-L}).\n4. Compute the product\n[\n\\prod_n A(\\theta_n)^2.\n]\n5. Compare with\n[\n3^{-L}\n]\nup to polynomial factors.\n\nMake tables:\n\n[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n\tL & \\max|\\widehat E_c(u)|^2 & 3^{-L} & \\text{ratio}\\\n\t\\hline\n\\end{array}\n]\n\nand:\n\n[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\tL & \\mathbb E[N_{\\rm weak}] & \\max N_{\\rm weak}\\text{ weighted}\\\n\t\\hline\n\\end{array}\n]\n\nYou are looking for ratios that grow polynomially in (c), not exponentially in (L).\n\n## 10. A proof skeleton for op:flat\n\nHere is the proof I would try to write.\n\n### Step 1: Level decomposition\n\nWrite:\n\n[\n\\sum_u|\\widehat E_c(u)|^2\n=========================\n\n\\sum_{L=0}^c\n\\sum_{\\operatorname{lev}(u)=L}\n|\\widehat E_c(u)|^2.\n]\n\n### Step 2: Fourier-recursion / phase-chain representation\n\nProve a representation:\n\n[\n|\\widehat E_c(u)|^2\n\\le\n\\mathbb E\\left[\n\\prod_{j=0}^{c-1} a(\\theta_j)^2\n\\right]\n]\n\nwith\n\n[\n\\theta_{j+1}=3\\cdot2^{b_j}\\theta_j\\pmod1.\n]\n\nOr get the exact equality if possible.\n\n### Step 3: Level transience\n\nProve that for initial level (L),\n\n[\n\\mathbb E\\left[\n\\prod_{j=0}^{c-1} a(\\theta_j)^2\n\\right]\n\\le\nC c^A3^{-L}.\n]\n\nThis is the main estimate.\n\n### Step 4: Sum levels\n\nSince level (L) has (\\asymp3^L) frequencies,\n\n[\n\\sum_{\\operatorname{lev}(u)=L}|\\widehat E_c(u)|^2\n\\le\nCc^A.\n]\n\nSumming (L\\le c) gives:\n\n[\n\\sum_u|\\widehat E_c(u)|^2\\le Cc^{A+1}.\n]\n\nThat proves op:flat.\n\n## 11. Manuscript endpoint\n\nThe paper should now say:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{finite H23a is reduced to op:flat, an annealed Fourier-decay estimate.}\n}\n]\n\nThen the proof chain:\n\n[\n\\operatorname{op{:}flat}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{cascade row contraction}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{threshold Schur}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\sup_c\\gamma_c(0)<1.\n]\n\nAnd explicitly:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{op:flat is independent of }\\chi,\\text{ coboundaries, and Collatz orbit determinism.}\n}\n]\n\nThis is important. It shows that the remaining finite-side theorem is not the global quenched obstruction. It is an annealed renewal estimate.\n\n## 12. Bottom line\n\nThe new endpoint is excellent:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{Everything finite-side now rests on }\n\t\\sum_u|\\widehat E_c(u)|^2\\le\\operatorname{poly}(c).\n}\n]\n\nThe natural attack is exactly the transience of the expanding phase chain:\n\n[\n\\theta\\mapsto3\\cdot2^b\\theta.\n]\n\nThe key theorem to prove is levelwise Fourier decay:\n\n[\n|\\widehat E_c(u)|\\le \\operatorname{poly}(c)3^{-L/2}\n]\n\nfor conductor level (L). Once that is proved, op:flat follows immediately.\n\nThis is now a clean, standalone analytic problem. It is not Collatz-specific in the old sense; it is a (3)-adic renewal Fourier decay estimate.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_25.md","numLines":580,"startLine":1,"totalLines":580},"type":"text"}